\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The dual tetrahedral field chamber. Four million points are sampled uniformly in local frame coordinates and tested against the half-space inequalities $L_i(\mathbf  {p})$ defined in the text. Points that satisfy the TetA bounds are shown in cyan; those satisfying TetB in red; and those inside both (the shared octahedral Field Origin) in gold. No mesh or curve fitting is used. Each face of TetA is subdivided into three exposed corners and one inverted core where the opposing vertex penetrates, giving $4 \times 4 = 16$ geometric channels. These channels determine the bimetric interaction potential. An interactive reconstruction is provided as supplementary material.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig1}{{1}{2}{The dual tetrahedral field chamber. Four million points are sampled uniformly in local frame coordinates and tested against the half-space inequalities $L_i(\mathbf {p})$ defined in the text. Points that satisfy the TetA bounds are shown in cyan; those satisfying TetB in red; and those inside both (the shared octahedral Field Origin) in gold. No mesh or curve fitting is used. Each face of TetA is subdivided into three exposed corners and one inverted core where the opposing vertex penetrates, giving $4 \times 4 = 16$ geometric channels. These channels determine the bimetric interaction potential. An interactive reconstruction is provided as supplementary material}{figure.1}{}}
